From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 22:02:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] kk: commit residency additions where they are made, removals
 in batches

KosmicKrisp committed its one device residency set, and requested its
residency, at every queue submit. In The Witcher 3's Metal System Trace
that is 4,666 commits in 15.8 s (16 a frame) on the vkd3d queue threads,
236 of them over 1 ms, and two of 212 and 229 ms are its two worst
frames.

On a Mac (M1 Pro, macOS 27.2, a 500-allocation set) a commit after an
addition costs about 28 us per MiB added, a commit after removals or
without a change under 3 us, and a removed allocation stays alive, held
by the set, until the next commit.

So an addition is now committed (and residency requested) where it is
made, before kk_alloc_bo or an import returns, on the thread that
allocates. A submit that uses the allocation was called after that, so it
needs no commit. Removals wait: a submit commits them once they hold
64 MiB or the oldest is 1 s old, and any addition's commit takes them
too. That keeps freed memory resident and alive a little longer, never
an allocation in use too short. A submit does not wait for another
thread's commit. When Metal gives no heap, kk_alloc_bo commits waiting
removals, which releases them, and tries once more.

MESA_KK_RESIDENCY=submit restores the commit at every submit, and with
MESA_KK_BATCH2's residency item the commit only after a change
(patches/mesa 0091), to compare. MESA_KK_DEBUG=memory logs
[kk-residency] about once a second: additions, removals and commits in
the interval, their total and longest time, those over 1 ms, and the
removals waiting.

Linear: PLA-465
Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-29-kk-residency-commits.md
Offered-upstream: not yet
